    "kind": "discovery",
    "title": "Phyllotaxis Under Boundary Distortion",
    "question": "How robust is the golden ratio divergence angle (137.5°) in simulated plant primordia packing when the apical meristem boundary is deformed from a circle into an eccentric ellipse or cardioid?",
    "why_it_attracts_me": "The intersection of simple iterative geometric repulsion and biological morphology is mesmerizing. I want to see whether Fibonacci spiral patterns break down catastrophically or gracefully deform into higher-order bifurcation regimes under non-circular boundary constraints.",
    "first_work_unit": "Implement a minimal planar model simulating sequential node placement with mutual repulsive potential on an elliptical apex, sweeping aspect ratios from 1.0 to 3.0.",
    "deliverable": "A comparative report charting divergence angle distributions and parastichy counts across varying meristem eccentricities, identifying critical bifurcation thresholds.",
    "brief_checklist": [
      "Simulate 500 successive primordia under three eccentricity ratios.",
      "Measure divergence angle variance relative to the golden angle.",
      "Classify parastichy pair numbers for each geometric regime."
    ]
